Cached CPU mappings of resource BOs (panfrost GL driver)

Mesa 26.2.4 has the buffer-object flag WB_MMAP ("write-back", i.e. cached
CPU mapping on a GPU that is not coherent with the CPU caches) and the
cache maintenance for it in the kmod layer (pan_kmod_queue_bo_map_sync,
pan_kmod_flush_bo_map_syncs; Linux' panthor >= 1.7), but only the Vulkan
driver uses them. The GL driver maps every BO non-cacheable, which makes
each CPU read of a BO slow -- on Genode the copy of the finished frame at
swap.

This patch lets the GL driver ask for WB_MMAP for the BOs of textures and
render targets with a linear or u-interleaved layout and adds the cache
maintenance where the CPU accesses them: panfrost_ptr_map/unmap (direct
mapping, tiled load/store, staging resource, shadow copy). Buffers
(PIPE_BUFFER), persistently mapped resources, AFBC/AFRC resources and all
BOs that are no resources (pools: command streams, descriptors, uploads,
shaders) stay non-cacheable.

PAN_WB_MMAP=0 switches the cached mappings off, PAN_BO_SYNC_KERNEL=1 lets
the kernel driver do the cache maintenance (BO_SYNC) instead of the CPU's
cache instructions in the process.

The hunk in panthor_kmod.c: the Gpu session of Genode's panthor port
reports the driver version 1.7 for its buffer-object interface on top of a
1.5 kernel driver, which lacks what 1.6 stands for (GLB_COUNTER_EN).

